    "Provenance, Reader Marginalia, Advertisements (1875, 1882) & Preface (1870) (pp. i–xxii, PDF pp. 1–23): Digitized from the University of Toronto Library copy (Barcode 3 1761 00101630 2, Call No. PL 8702 S724 1894, stamped JAN 9 1970, microformed AUG - 9 1989), with pencil title inscription 'CHUO CHA KISWAHILI', 3 chromolithographed floral plates (PDF pp. 10, 262, 448), and manuscript annotations on p. 6 (Arabic ligature امغزف illustrating the Kilwa letter amekufa 'had died' vs. amevuka 'had got away'), p. 429 (5-line cursive Swahili note 'Bwana wangu ninyemheshimu sana...'), and p. 453 (vocalized Ajami transcription of Phrase 197 سَلَامُ سَانَ حَجَ يَاغُ يَكُعِشِ وِوِ سَانَ نَفُرَاحَ). The Advertisement to the 2nd Edition (p. xv, Jan. 1875) drops the old 2nd class of substantives because all animate nouns take Class I concords; the Advertisement to the 3rd Edition (pp. xvii–xix, Christmas 1882, signed A. C. M.) notes Bishop Steere spent some of the last hours of his life revising the Handbook, adds Appendix V with Ven. Archdeacon Hodgson's approval, and notes Dr. Krapf's 1882 Dictionary arrived too late for the Bishop's use. The Preface to the 1st Edition (pp. iii–xiii, May 1870) chronicles Steere's arrival in Zanzibar with Bishop Tozer (end of Aug. 1864), early vocabularies (Krapf, Rebmann, Witt & Schultz, von der Decken & Kersten, Wakefield), informants Hamis wa Tani & his son Mohammed bin Khamis, Hamis wa Kayi, Sheikh 'Abd al 'Aziz (Psalms 1–16), Sheikh Mohammed bin Ali, Hassan bin Yusuf, Rev. & Mrs. G. E. Drayton, Rev. W. & Mrs. Lea, critique of Père Etienne Baur's Catechism, collections in Shambala, Yao, Nyamwezi, Gindo, Zaramo & Ngazidja, and the jesting folk etymology of Swahili from Sawa hila ('All same cheat', p. xiii) vs. Arabic Sawáhil (coasts).",
    "Part I — Introduction, Alphabet, Phonology & Classes I–VIII (pp. 1–82): Introduction (pp. 1–7) situates Swahili in Bleek's Zangian genus, critiques Frédoux's Sechuana class numbering, documents the 3 rules of broken 'foreigners' Swahili' (forming all plurals with ma- or nyingi, conjugating all verbs with na-/ma-/ta-, and negating everything with hakuna, pp. 3–4), and recounts the Kilwa Arabic-script letter ambiguity (amekufa vs. amevuka, p. 6). The Alphabet (pp. 8–15) distinguishes italic th (voiceless, Arabic ث) from roman th (voiced, Arabic ذ/ظ/ض), velar nasal ng' vs. prenasalized ng, explosive aspirated p', t', k' caused by a suppressed n prefix in Class VI plurals (upepo → p'epo, upanga → p'anga, ukuta → k'uta, p. 11), latent intervocalic l dropped in Zanzibar but preserved in Merima (Merima kufala = Zanzibar kufaa) and resurgent when -ni shifts stress (mzambarau → Mzambaraúni, p. 13), and open-syllable division proven by Kinyume (p. 14). Substantives (pp. 16–82) cover Classes I–VIII, the 3 locative concords of -ni (mw- inside, p- near, kw- to/from/at, pp. 21–22), and categorized English–Swahili domain lists: 9 Antelopes (p. 25), 14 Baskets & Matting Bags (pp. 26–27), 6 Cakes (p. 30), 26 Coconut terms (pp. 33–34), 8 Dhow types (p. 38), 5 Dollar coins (p. 39), Dress (p. 40), Fish (p. 43), 6 Games (p. 45), 3 Handfuls (p. 47: ukufi, chopa, konzi), Dr. Krapf's Delay Proverb (p. 48), 11 Musical Instruments (p. 57), 10 Rice Varieties (p. 65), Seasons & 3 Shamba Servants (p. 68: Msimamizi, Nokoa, Kadamu), 7 Evil Spirits (p. 71), Divisions of Time (pp. 75–77: 12 lunar months, 365-day solar/nautical year from Siku a mwaka / Kigunzi in 10-day miongo decades, daakuu 2:30 A.M. gun, 7 weekdays & 18 prayer/daily hours incl. Mafungulia ng'ombe makuu [8 A.M.] and madogo [9 A.M.]), Weights (pp. 80–81), 8 Woods (p. 82), and Wahadimu & Munyi mkuu of Zanzibar (p. 82).",
    "Part I — Adjectives, Numerals, 16 Dollar Fractions, Pronouns, Verbs & 9-Language Comparative Tables (pp. 83–235): Adjectives & Numerals (pp. 83–101) detail vowel-initial prefix fusion, 5 ways to supply missing adjectives, Comparison (kuliko, zayidi ya, punde, kupita, kuzidi/kupungua), Bantu & Arabic numerals 1–1,000,000 (mosi–kumi, fungate [7], mwongo [10], kikwi [1,000], lakki [100,000], milyón), the 8-Class Numeral Agreement Table (pp. 91–92), and all 16 Fractions of a Dollar (p. 93: Nuss ya themuni 1/16, Themuni/Ze'mni 1/8, Zerenge 1/5, Robo 1/4, Nuss 1/2, Kassa robo 3/4, Kassa themuni 7/8, Kassa nuss ya themuni 15/16). Pronouns (pp. 102–124) cover Na- contractions (nami–nao), poetic enclitic object suffixes (p. 108: hukunazo, umenifanyiziaje), enclitic possessives (mwanangu, mumewe, mkeo, jinalo, shogaye), 3 Demonstrative series (huyu/huyo/yule), Copular ndimi–ndiko & negative siye–sicho (pp. 115–116), and Relatives (-ye-/-o-, suffixal ampendaye, negative asiyependa, and -vyo/-po/-ko/-mo). Verbs (pp. 125–208) present all affirmative and negative tenses, nika- → ha- and niki- → hi- contractions, Madan's bracketed note on p. 142 on preposed intensive infinitives (kufa utakufa) and coordinated infinitives (anayeabudiwa na kutukuzwa), 4 Derivative Forms (Applied, Causative, Neuter -ka, Reciprocal -ana & -kana), and the List of Verbs (pp. 165–208). Followed by Adverbs, Prepositions, Conjunctions, Salutations & 24 Interjections (pp. 209–225), Word-Formation (pp. 226–233), and the Comparative Tables of Noun Prefixes in 9 Bantu Languages (p. 234: Swahili, Shambala, [Ki]Nyika, [Ki]Kamba, Nyamwezi, Yao, Kafir, [Se]Chuana, [Otyi]Herero) and Preposition -a in 4 Languages (p. 235).",
    "Part II — Preliminary Observations, Swahili–English Vocabulary (18 Kanzu Parts & 41 Zanzibar Mita) & Appendices I–V (pp. 237–458): Preliminary Observations (pp. 239–243) give 12 stem-stripping rules, 4 Prefix Reference Tables (17 substantival, 18 adjectival, 18 pronominal, 47 verbal prefixes), and the Coastal Dialect Sound-Correspondence Table (p. 243: Zanzibar ch = t [M.], e = a [M.], h = kh [Ar.], j = d [M.] / y [A., N.], t = ch [Ozi.], v = z [A.], z = v [Mer.] / th [Patta & Ozi] / j [vulgar]). The Swahili–English Vocabulary (pp. 245–421, ~5,500 headwords) documents all 18 Named Parts of a Man's Kanzu (s.v. Kanzu, p. 299), all 41 Named Mita of Zanzibar Town (s.v. Mta, pp. 346–347: 30 Peninsula quarters + 11 Ng'ambo quarters), the $2 Muhadimu house-tax shared between Seyed Majid and the Munyi mkuu at Dunga (p. 349), and embedded proverbs & bird calls (pp. 262, 268, 287, 301, 311, 312, 322, 333, 341, 352). Appendices I–V (pp. 423–458) comprise: Appendix I (all 52 Merima-dialect Kinyume pairs by Johari, pp. 425–426, where Mguru → Rumgu and Mwanamuke → Kemwanamu); Appendix II (2 Prose Letters & 2 Poetic Epistles incl. 'Risala enda kwa hima / Kwa Edward Istira...', pp. 427–429); Appendix III (interlinear tale 'Hadithi ya Waridi, na Ureda, na Jindi, na Sinébar', pp. 431–442); Appendix IV (all 197 Useful & Idiomatic Phrases, pp. 443–453); and Appendix V (Money [47 dollars = 100 rupees], Lengths [Dunga = saa 'nne, Kokotoni = saa sita], Weights, Capacities [Kwale/Mgao/Mvita vs. Pangani/Tanga/Mtang'ata kanda tables], and the 32-Point Compass on p. 458)."
